{
  "version": 2,
  "cells": [
    {
      "kind": "markup",
      "language": "markdown",
      "value": [
        "# Go\n",
        "\n",
        "Go fits the notebook model with one wrinkle worth knowing: **`go build` refuses a\n",
        "file list that spans directories**. Every cell whose file lands in the build dir\n",
        "root goes on the command line; a sub-directory is a separate package, which Go\n",
        "expects to resolve by *import* instead. The second project below shows that.\n",
        "\n",
        "No `GOPATH`, no module, no scaffolding: a flat `package main` builds as is."
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "toml",
      "value": [
        "# go project. Every key is optional; these are the defaults.\n",
        "compiler = \"go\"\n",
        "mode     = \"run\"\n",
        "output   = \"greeter\"\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "go",
      "value": [
        "package main\n",
        "\n",
        "import \"fmt\"\n",
        "\n",
        "// Same package as main.go, just a different cell. Both files sit in the build\n",
        "// dir root, so both are named on the go build command line.\n",
        "func Greet(name string) string {\n",
        "\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"hello, %s, from a compiler notebook\", name)\n",
        "}\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "role": "file",
        "filename": "greet.go"
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "go",
      "value": [
        "package main\n",
        "\n",
        "import \"fmt\"\n",
        "\n",
        "func main() {\n",
        "\tfor _, name := range []string{\"world\", \"go\"} {\n",
        "\t\tfmt.Println(Greet(name))\n",
        "\t}\n",
        "}\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "role": "file",
        "filename": "main.go"
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "markup",
      "language": "markdown",
      "value": [
        "## A module, for more than one package\n",
        "\n",
        "Once a project wants a *second package*, it needs a `go.mod` — that is what turns\n",
        "the build dir into a module and makes `demo/greet` importable. `go.mod` is written\n",
        "by an **asset cell**: any language, but explicitly named, so it is copied into the\n",
        "build dir and never handed to the compiler.\n",
        "\n",
        "Only `main.go` is on the command line here. `greet/greet.go` is reached by import,\n",
        "the same way Rust reaches a `mod` and Zig an `@import`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "toml",
      "value": [
        "compiler = \"go\"\n",
        "output = \"modular\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "go.mod",
      "value": [
        "module demo\n",
        "\n",
        "go 1.21\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "role": "file",
        "filename": "go.mod"
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "go",
      "value": [
        "package greet\n",
        "\n",
        "import \"fmt\"\n",
        "\n",
        "func Hello(name string) string {\n",
        "\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"hello, %s, from package greet\", name)\n",
        "}\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "role": "file",
        "filename": "greet/greet.go"
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "go",
      "value": [
        "package main\n",
        "\n",
        "import (\n",
        "\t\"fmt\"\n",
        "\n",
        "\t\"demo/greet\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "func main() {\n",
        "\tfmt.Println(greet.Hello(\"modules\"))\n",
        "}\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "role": "file",
        "filename": "main.go"
      }
    }
  ]
}
